{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Like a swallow [or] crane.</b> The kamatz of כְּסוּס draws after it its 'am [i.e., the kamatz is gadol], like כְּרֵעַ כְּאַח לִי (Psalms 31:14), and similarly כִּכְבַשׁ אָלוּף — and these are birds. The meaning: the sick person, in the severity of his illness, chirps and moans, and no one understands his words." "<b>דלו.</b> From the roots of doubling [i.e., hollow/נפעל roots], like דָּלוּ שׁוֹקִים (Proverbs 26:7), in the sense of lifting." "<b>YHWH, it is pressed for me.</b> This is an expression of pressing." "<b>Pledge me.</b> Now pledge Your servant for good (Psalms 119:122). Some say that the ayin stands in place of a chet; this is nonsense. The word ערבני is in the manner of human speech, and the meaning is: intermingle with me in my distress."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:56:55.992016193Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}